finish('constant-approximation-acyclic-reachable-pairs',
 id='TCS-0479',title='Can an acyclic subgraph preserve a constant fraction of the optimal reachable pairs?',
 area='Graph theory and graph algorithms',criterion='resources',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''For a finite directed graph G=(V,E), let $R(D)=|\{(u,v)\in V^2:u\ne v,\text{ a directed path from }u\text{ to }v\text{ exists in }D\}|$ and $\mathrm{OPT}(G)=\max\{R((V,F)):F\subseteq E,\ (V,F)\text{ is acyclic}\}$. Do there exist a constant alpha>0 and a deterministic algorithm running in time polynomial in |V|+|E| that, for every G, outputs F subset E with (V,F) acyclic and R((V,F)) >= alpha OPT(G)? The constant and polynomial exponent must be independent of G. This card selects constant-factor approximation as a precise target within the source’s broader optimization question.''',
 definitions='The input is an adjacency-list representation of an unweighted directed graph with labeled vertices 1,...,n, no self-loops, and at most one edge for each ordered pair. Oppositely directed edges may both be present. The algorithm may delete edges but may not add edges, reverse edges, identify vertices, or remove vertices. Reachability uses paths of positive length and counts ordered pairs of distinct vertices, not paths or retained edges. An acyclic graph has no directed cycle. Output is the list of retained edges. Polynomial time is measured in ordinary bit operations on the finite input; no oracle for reachability or optimization is free. When OPT(G)=0, any acyclic output satisfies the value requirement.',
 answer_criterion='Give a polynomial-time algorithm and prove one universal positive approximation ratio, or prove that no algorithm with the quantified guarantee exists. A conditional impossibility result must identify its complexity assumption. Guarantees on the number or weight of deleted edges, or empirical similarity to a reference hierarchy, do not establish the required guarantee on reachable pairs.',
 context=[
  paragraph('A directed edge can represent a declared relation between two concepts, while a path represents an implied relation. Removing one edge can therefore erase many implications even if almost every input edge remains. The objective counts the implications preserved after all cycles have been removed. The optimum in the denominator already obeys acyclicity, so the algorithm is not asked to retain mutually contradictory reachability in both directions.'),
  paragraph('For example, a directed path on n vertices has only n-1 edges but n(n-1)/2 reachable ordered pairs. Deleting a middle edge destroys roughly a quarter of all n^2 ordered pairs. Conversely, adding a shortcut between vertices that are already connected by a path increases the edge count without adding a new reachable pair. These examples explain why edge-count approximation guarantees cannot simply be reused for this objective.'),
  paragraph('Ajwani’s 2016 question asks for an acyclic edge subgraph whose transitive closure has maximum size, motivated by cleaning taxonomies assembled from multiple contributors. The source explicitly welcomes approximation algorithms and scalable heuristics but specifies no approximation threshold. The constant alpha target here is an editorial specialization, rather than an approximation conjecture quoted from the seminar.', 'seminar'),
  paragraph('The 2017 follow-up on noisy hierarchies develops practical cycle-removal heuristics and evaluates recovery of inserted noisy edges. It identifies deviation in transitive closure as a possible further measure. Those experiments do not provide a worst-case ratio for R(D). A 2026 ranking paper likewise uses reachability to make edge reinsertion cycle-safe, but optimizes edge-weight disagreement; that use of reachability does not turn its objective into this one.', 'heuristics')],
 why='This isolates how much implied relational information can be retained when a cyclic dependency system must become a partial order. It is a structural approximation problem in which the value of an edge depends on all paths that use it, and it asks for a guarantee on that global effect.',
 importance=dict(score=72,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='A clean graph-optimization direction connecting acyclicity with preservation of global reachability. The constant-factor target captures a fundamental guarantee missing from the cited heuristic work, though its literature is narrower than core shortest-path and dynamic-graph frontiers.'),
 progress=[
  step('2016','Ajwani formulates the transitive-closure maximization objective for acyclic subgraphs and asks for approximation algorithms as well as practical heuristics.','seminar'),
  step('2017','Sun and coauthors study cycle removal in noisy hierarchies experimentally. Their conclusion proposes transitive-closure deviation as another measure to investigate; no constant-factor theorem for the reachable-pair objective is supplied.','heuristics'),
  step('2026-09-09','Vahidi studies ranking through weighted feedback-arc removal and exact cycle-safe reinsertion. The objective and stated guarantees concern edge weights and ranking inconsistency, so they do not settle this approximation target.','ranking')],
 references=[
  ref('seminar','Data Structures and Advanced Models of Computation on Big Data (Dagstuhl Seminar 16101)','Deepak Ajwani (problem contributor)',2016,'https://doi.org/10.4230/DagRep.6.3.1','Section 4.1'),
  ref('heuristics','Breaking Cycles in Noisy Hierarchies','Jiankai Sun; Deepak Ajwani; Patrick K. Nicholson; Alessandra Sala; Srinivasan Parthasarathy',2017,'https://doi.org/10.1145/3091478.3091495','WebSci 2017; experimental objectives and Section 5 future work; author manuscript in the UCD research repository'),
  ref('ranking','Training-free ranking from pairwise comparisons via acyclic graph construction','Soroush Vahidi',2026,'https://doi.org/10.1007/s11227-026-08852-4','Definitions 1–2 and Sections 1.1–1.2: edge-weight objectives and guarantee scope')],
 status='source_open',status_note='The original optimization direction is open in the 2016 source. This card explicitly specializes it to deterministic constant-factor approximation. Searches through 10 September 2026 did not locate a ratio theorem or impossibility result for this exact reachable-pair objective; cited feedback-arc and hierarchy heuristics use different guarantees.',
 formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
